      "No third-party certification we could confirm"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The easiest lemon clear whey to buy with Prime shipping; a bit cheaper than 1UP and no better tested.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "20 g (listing)",
      "Calories": "About 100-110",
      "Third-party testing": "Brand claim; no cert confirmed",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $2.78 (Amazon price seen)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DHT1K42Y"
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "1UP Protein Powder Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/1up-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is 1UP protein third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not by an independent certifier that we could find. 1UP says its products are made in an NSF/ANSI 455-2 GMP certified, FDA-registered facility in Miami and that every batch is lab tested for accuracy, purity, heavy metals and microbes. But we found no 1UP powder in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases, and 1UP does not publish batch results."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is 1UP protein powder healthy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is a clean, low-calorie protein for most healthy adults. ISO Protein has 90 calories, 20 g of protein and 0 g of sugar per scoop, and Clear Protein has 80 calories with zero carbs, per the labels. The ingredient lists are short. It is a supplement, not a meal, so it works best alongside a balanced diet rather than in place of food."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does 1UP protein contain lactose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "1UP Clear Protein is described by the brand as lactose free, because its filtering removes lactose. ISO Protein is whey isolate, which is very low in lactose but not labeled lactose free, so very sensitive people may still react. 1UP Vegan Protein has no dairy ingredients but is made on equipment that also processes milk, per the label."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which 1UP protein flavor is best?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buyers most often name Lemon Italian Ice as the best Clear Protein flavor, with Strawberry Italian Ice and Sour Cherry close behind. For ISO Protein, buyer reviews favor Salted Caramel (the Sydney Adams collaboration), Coconut Ice Cream and Birthday Cake. For Vegan Protein, Banana Bread gets the most praise. This reflects buyer feedback, not our own tasting."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the most common complaints about 1UP protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Price is the biggest one: about $3.00 to $3.41 per 25 g at list price. Some buyers report an aftertaste, especially in stevia and monk fruit flavors, and clear protein foams when shaken. Vegan buyers mention a gritty texture. A few also notice the scoop now delivers 20 g of protein rather than the 25 g older reviews describe."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is 1UP protein powder sold on Amazon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We could not find 1UP protein powders on Amazon when we searched in November 2026. 1UP sells them through 1upnutrition.com, where bundles and sales bring the price down. If you want to stay on Amazon, Dymatize ISO100 Birthday Cake and Oath Clear Whey Frosty Lemonade are the closest matches we could verify."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is 1UP Birthday Cake protein sweetened with stevia?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. 1UP sells ISO Protein Birthday Cake only in its stevia and monk fruit version, which 1UP calls 1UPSweet, per the label at the time of writing. A scoop gives 20 g of whey isolate protein, 90 calories and 0 g of sugar. There is no sucralose version of Birthday Cake, unlike most other ISO flavors."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "1up protein powder",
      "1up nutrition protein powder",
      "1up birthday cake protein powder",
      "1up clear protein",
      "1up lemon italian ice protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Picks for Women",
    link: "/best-protein-powder-for-women/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">1UP Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For anyone eyeing 1UP Nutrition's ISO, Clear or Vegan protein: what the current labels say, what the testing claim covers, what each tub costs per 25 g, and the Amazon swaps if you would rather not order direct.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#lineup">The 1UP Lineup</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#iso-label">ISO Protein Label</a>
      <a href="#clear-label">Clear Protein Label</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">1UP vs Bloom and Alani</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          1UP protein powder is a clean, low-calorie label at a high price: every current 1UP powder gives 20 g of protein per scoop for 80 to 100 calories, at about $3.00 to $3.41 per 25 g from 1upnutrition.com. What changes the verdict is testing and budget. 1UP lab-tests its own batches but holds no NSF or Informed Sport product certification. Below: labels, testing, cost, buyer feedback and alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 1UP Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            1UP protein powder is a well-labeled but expensive line. Every powder 1UP currently sells gives 20 g of protein per scoop for 80 to 100 calories with 0 g of sugar, per the supplement facts on its site at the time of writing, and you can pick a stevia and monk fruit version of most flavors. The catch is price: about $3.00 to $3.41 per 25 g of protein, against roughly $1.60 to $2.10 for comparable isolates. 1UP also has no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport product listing that we could find.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy 1UP if</strong> you want a low-calorie isolate or clear whey, you care about flavor variety (Birthday Cake, Lemon Italian Ice and Salted Caramel all get strong buyer reviews) or you want to avoid sucralose without giving up dessert flavors. <strong>Skip 1UP if</strong> you are budget-driven, you are a drug-tested athlete who needs a certified product, or you want to order from Amazon, where we could not find 1UP powders listed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">1UP by the Numbers (label, at the time of writing)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop, every current 1UP powder</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">80-100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$3.00-3.41</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost per 25 g on 1upnutrition.com</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">NSF Sport or Informed Sport listings found</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which 1UP Protein Powders Can You Buy Right Now?
          </h2>

          <p>
            1UP Nutrition sells three protein powders today: ISO Protein (a whey isolate), Clear Protein Isolate and Vegan Protein. A fourth, 100% Egg White Protein, was listed but sold out in every flavor when we checked 1upnutrition.com in November 2026. The older 1UP Whey Protein, the blend many reviews and food-tracking apps still mention, no longer has a live product page, so if you search for "1UP whey protein" and land on ISO, that is why.
          </p>

          <p>
            1UP is a Miami company that makes and tests its products in its own facility, per the brand. It is often described as a women's fitness brand, and its marketing leans that way: there are "for Her" fat-loss and muscle stacks, a Her BCAA formula and a long list of female athlete collaborations, such as the Sydney Adams Salted Caramel flavor. The protein powders themselves are not formulated for women. 1UP tags them for both men and women, and the labels have nothing sex-specific in them, which is normal and fine. Protein needs depend on body weight and training, not on gender marketing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flavor list is where 1UP stands out. ISO Protein comes in 13 options, including Birthday Cake, Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream, Coconut Ice Cream, Cinnamon Dolce Latte and Chocolate Peanut Butter Blast, and most of them are sold twice: once sweetened with sucralose and once with 1UP's own stevia and monk fruit blend, which it calls 1UPSweet. Clear Protein has 11 options, led by Lemon Italian Ice, Strawberry Italian Ice and Sour Cherry. Vegan Protein has six, two of which were out of stock.
          </p>

          <p>
            The search phrase "1UP birthday cake protein powder" now points to one product: <strong>ISO Protein in Birthday Cake, sweetened with stevia and monk fruit</strong>. There is no sucralose Birthday Cake. "1UP lemon Italian ice protein powder" can mean two products, because Lemon Italian Ice is both a Clear Protein flavor (in sucralose and stevia versions) and a Vegan Protein flavor, which was sold out at the time of writing. Check which line you are adding to the cart; the clear version is a juice-style drink, and the vegan version is a creamy shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            1UP sells mainly through its own store. We searched Amazon for "1UP Nutrition whey", "1 Up Nutrition clear protein" and "1UP protein powder birthday cake" and found no 1UP powders, only other brands. That is why the product cards below include two Amazon alternatives, and why the 1UP cards are priced from 1UP's site rather than from a retailer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. 1UP Powders and the Closest Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            1UP sells its protein through its own store, and we could not find it listed on Amazon. The first three cards are 1UP products priced from 1upnutrition.com. The last two are the closest Amazon alternatives we could verify: a birthday cake whey isolate and a lemonade clear whey. Cost per 25 g uses the label's servings and protein and the price we saw.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="iso-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in 1UP ISO Protein (and the Birthday Cake Flavor)?
          </h2>

          <p>
            1UP ISO Protein gives 20 g of protein in a 26 g scoop, with 90 calories, 0.5 g of fat, 1 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar, per the Vanilla Ice Cream supplement facts panel on 1UP's <a href="https://1upnutrition.com/products/iso-protein" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">ISO Protein page</a> at the time of writing. The panel lists 24 g of whey protein isolate "delivering 20 g complete protein" and 25 servings per container. The ingredient list is short: whey protein isolate, sunflower lecithin, natural flavors and sucralose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Birthday Cake is the stevia version, and its panel is nearly identical. Per that label, a 26.5 g scoop gives the same 20 g of protein and 90 calories, 1 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar, sweetened with 1UPSweet (stevia and monk fruit extract) and a little sodium chloride. The salt is the one real difference: Birthday Cake lists 120 mg of sodium per scoop against 45 mg for the sucralose Vanilla Ice Cream. The tub front says "Flavored / No Artificial Colors", and the label has no sprinkles or candy pieces.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per scoop (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">ISO Vanilla Ice Cream (sucralose)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">ISO Birthday Cake (stevia + monk fruit)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scoop size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbs / sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g / 0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g / 0 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">45 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia + monk fruit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Servings per tub</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25</td>
