          </div>

          <p>
            One thing to watch: 1UP's own product page still says "25g Protein" in its headline copy, and a highlighted buyer review mentions 110 calories and 25 g per scoop. The current tubs and supplement facts panels say 20 g and 90 calories. The likeliest explanation is that the scoop got smaller when the tubs moved to the 25-serving size (the image files are labeled "new size"). Go by the panel on the tub in your hand, not by the headline, and if you are comparing with older reviews, expect less protein per scoop than they describe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergen-wise, ISO Protein contains milk and is made on equipment that also processes wheat, eggs, soy, fish, tree nuts, sesame and peanuts, per the label. 1UP's customer service has said in its on-site Q&A that the ISO whey contains no soy ingredients and that most whey flavors are gluten free, with the exception of cookie flavors that use wheat flour. None of the current ISO flavors are cookie flavors, but anyone with celiac disease or a serious allergy should treat the shared-equipment warning as real and talk to their doctor before relying on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clear-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in 1UP Clear Protein (Lemon Italian Ice)?
          </h2>

          <p>
            1UP Clear Protein Isolate gives 20 g of protein in a 27 g scoop with 80 calories, 0 g of fat, 0 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar, per the Lemon Italian Ice supplement facts on 1UP's <a href="https://1upnutrition.com/products/clear-protein" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Clear Protein page</a> at the time of writing. The tub holds 22 servings, three fewer than ISO Protein. The ingredients are clear whey protein isolate, citric acid, natural flavor, tartaric acid, turmeric powder for the yellow color and sucralose. A stevia and monk fruit version of Lemon Italian Ice is also listed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear whey is whey isolate that has been acidified and filtered further so it dissolves into a thin, juice-like drink instead of a milky shake. 1UP says its version goes through ultrafiltration, cross-flow microfiltration and ion exchange, which it says strips out lactose, fat, carbs and sugar, and it describes the product as lactose free. The 0 g carbohydrate line on the label is consistent with that claim, since lactose is a carbohydrate. If you want to understand how this compares with other clear wheys, our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide compares the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sodium is higher than in the ISO sucralose flavors, at 125 mg per scoop, and potassium is 281 mg, per the label. Neither number matters much for most people, but they are worth knowing if you track sodium for blood pressure. The acids give clear whey its tart, sports-drink taste and are also why some people find clear protein harsh on an empty stomach or on their teeth if they sip it slowly over hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            1UP's directions are specific: add 10 oz of cold water first, then the powder, shake for 30 seconds and let it sit 30 to 45 seconds before pouring over ice. It foams a lot at first, which 1UP calls normal. Mixing it with milk defeats the purpose and can curdle, because of the acids. Buyers who skip the rest step are the ones most likely to complain about a frothy texture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is 1UP Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            1UP protein is lab tested, but it is not third-party certified by NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport as far as we could find. 1UP states that its products are made in an "NSF/ANSI cGMP 455-2 Certified and FDA-Registered Facility in Miami FL" and that every batch is tested for formula accuracy, potency and purity, plus heavy metals, microbes and pathogens. We found no 1UP products in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport searches at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those claims are not the same thing as a product certification. An NSF GMP facility registration means an auditor has checked that the plant follows good manufacturing practice: clean equipment, records, procedures. It does not mean NSF has tested the powder in the tub. Programs like NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport test the finished product for banned substances and, in NSF's case, check that the label matches what is inside, and they publish the results by product and often by lot number.
          </p>

          <p>
            1UP also does not publish its batch results or name the lab that runs them, so you are taking the brand's word for the heavy metal testing. That does not mean anything is wrong with the product. Most protein brands are in the same position, and whey isolate tends to carry less lead and cadmium risk than plant proteins grown in soil. But if heavy metals are your main concern, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide favors brands that publish results or hold a certification.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A certified facility is not a certified product.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you are tested for banned substances in sport, choose a product you can look up by name in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database. 1UP's facility claim does not cover you. Dymatize ISO100, the Birthday Cake alternative below, is Informed Choice certified, per Dymatize.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For most people buying 1UP for general fitness or weight loss, the testing question comes down to trust. 1UP is a long-running brand with tens of thousands of on-site reviews and an in-house manufacturing setup, which gives it more control than brands that outsource. The honest summary is: tested by the brand, not verified by an independent certifier.
          </p>
        </div>

