        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy 1UP Protein, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            1UP protein suits people who want a low-calorie isolate or clear whey with lots of flavors and are willing to pay for it. If you are in a calorie deficit and want 20 g of protein for 80 or 90 calories, both ISO and Clear deliver that, per their labels. If you avoid sucralose but still want Birthday Cake or Salted Caramel, the stevia and monk fruit versions are a real advantage. And if you are lactose sensitive, 1UP Clear is described by the brand as lactose free; our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> guide has more options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip 1UP if price per gram matters. At roughly $3.25 per 25 g, a year of one daily 20 g scoop of ISO costs about $950 at list price, while the same 20 g a day from Dymatize ISO100 costs roughly $470 at the Amazon price we saw. Skip it too if you compete in a drug-tested sport, because 1UP has no product-level certification you can look up. And if you want to buy in a store or on Amazon, 1UP is hard to find; our sibling guide to <a href="/sprouts-protein-powder/">Sprouts protein powder</a> covers what you can pick up in a grocery aisle instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Women who landed here because 1UP is marketed to them should know that any of these powders works the same way for women as for men. What matters is total protein for the day, roughly 1.4 to 2.0 g per kg of body weight a day for people who train regularly, per the International Society of Sports Nutrition's position stand on protein. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-women/">best protein powder for women</a> guide ranks options on label, testing and cost rather than on marketing.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few health notes. Pregnant or breastfeeding readers should check with their doctor first; 1UP's customer service gives the same advice in its Q&A, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-pregnancy/">protein powder for pregnancy</a> guide covers what to look for. People with kidney disease should not add protein supplements without medical advice. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid ISO and Clear, and anyone with a severe allergy to wheat, soy, peanuts or tree nuts should note that all 1UP powders are made on shared equipment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every 1UP scoop has 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                1UP's ISO page headline still says 25 g of protein, but the current supplement facts panels show 20 g per 26 g scoop. If you planned your daily protein around 25 g a scoop, you are 5 g short each time. Read the panel on your tub, which is what the protein total really is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the NSF facility claim as product certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                1UP's plant is NSF GMP registered, which audits manufacturing practice. It is not NSF Certified for Sport, which tests the finished product for banned substances and label accuracy. Athletes in tested sports should buy a product listed by name in a certifier's database, not one made in a certified facility.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering the wrong Lemon Italian Ice</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lemon Italian Ice is both a Clear Protein flavor and a Vegan Protein flavor at 1UP. The clear one is a tart, juice-like whey drink; the vegan one is a creamy pea and sunflower shake and was sold out when we checked. Look at the product name in the cart before paying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying from unverified third-party sellers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                We found no 1UP powders on Amazon. If a marketplace listing appears, check that it is sold by 1UP or an authorized retailer, because supplements from unknown resellers can be old, stored hot or not genuine. Ordering direct from 1upnutrition.com is the safest way to get a current tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking Clear Protein with milk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                1UP Clear contains citric and tartaric acid and is designed for cold water. Mixing it with milk can curdle and gives you a thick, sour drink. Add 10 oz of cold water first, the powder second, shake, and let the foam settle for 30 to 45 seconds before drinking.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

